# Test output. `.coverage` is the sqlite database a coverage run writes: a
# build artifact with no reader, carrying the absolute paths of whichever
# machine produced it. It was tracked until `publishable/04`, which is what an
# artifact with no ignore rule does — an untracked file comes back on the next
# careless `git add`.
coverage/
.coverage
.coverage.*
.gstack/

# No PDF belongs at the repository root. A third party's article was committed
# there and went unnoticed for weeks (`publishable/04`); republishing somebody
# else's copyrighted work is the reason that mattered, and a careless `git add`
# at the root is how it arrived. Anchored, so a PDF that a document genuinely
# needs can still live beside that document. `git add -f` is the hatch.
/*.pdf

# Code Workshop workflow — runtime jail and PR dry-run output, never sources
harness-loop-graph.pdf
.claude/settings.local.json
.dev/

# Runtime state, not content: thread checkpoints (ticket 05) live beside the
# workflow packages they are state about. The -wal/-shm siblings are sqlite's.
.openstategraph/
workflows/.openstategraph/
test-results/
playwright-report/

# Internal planning substrate — local only, never published
.scratch/
HANDOVER.md

# The refused-term table for `backend/tests/test_the_tree_is_publishable.py`.
# The gate is the one place a client engagement is named, and `publishable/06`
# measured what that cost: tracked, the gate would have published the list it
# exists to keep out, in the commit and in the sdist both. So the words live
# here, untracked, and the gate loads them — skipping loudly when the file is
# absent rather than reporting a green it did not earn. At the repository root
# on purpose: the sdist is built from `backend/`, so a table up here cannot
# reach a distribution even if this rule were deleted.
/.engagement-terms.json
